  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k-x,\\, & -1<x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(k-x)\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(k-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[kx-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-[-k-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =k+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk+\\frac{1}{2}=1\\,\u27f9\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$",
